{"date": "2026-11-17", "post_id": 1511784, "title": "Milky Way versus Aurora", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way Galaxy</a>, an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/planet-earth-through-the-ages/\">our Earth</a>\u00a0and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2004-november-12-missouris-green-ribbon-sky/\">auroral ribbon</a>\u00a0that is only hours old, composed of energized\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/articles/zc86m39#zygjdp3\">atoms and molecules</a>, and is close by -- right in\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/properties-of-earths-upper-atmosphere/\">Earth's upper atmosphere</a>. These chance-superposed\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-30-the-surface-of-titan-from-huygens/\">titan</a>s have\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-june-15-prominences-and-filaments-on-the-active-sun/\">our Sun</a>\u00a0in common, a\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/media?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpreview.redd.it%2Fthis-is-the-most-cute-thing-i-ever-seen-v0-mpx697lakcue1.jpg%3Fwidth%3D1080%26crop%3Dsmart%26auto%3Dwebp%26s%3Da5d18b145135737912b5caa61fcc56766726f6cb\">stellar member</a>\u00a0of the\u00a0Milky Way\u00a0that ejects energetic particles which create\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-10-in-green-company-aurora-over-norway/\">aurora</a>s. Far behind the middle of the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-january-13-arches-across-an-arctic-sky/\">auroral arch</a>\u00a0is the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/\">Big Dipper</a>. Even farther in the distance is the\u00a0<a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/11675/\">Andromeda galaxy</a>\u00a0-- the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfAZEgmIDIq/\">featured image</a>\u00a0was taken about a month ago from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/4VlzJh3WBGU\">Kval\u00f8ya</a>\u00a0in northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway\">Norway</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/guillaume_scheib/\">Guillaume Scheib</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/guillaume_scheib/\">Guillaume Scheib</a>", "alt": "A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a  smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 17 - Milky Way versus Aurora</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our\u00a0Milky Way Galaxy, an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates\u00a0our Earth\u00a0and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an\u00a0auroral ribbon\u00a0that is only hours old, composed of energized\u00a0atoms and molecules, and is close by -- right in\u00a0Earth&#039;s upper atmosphere. These chance-superposed\u00a0titans have\u00a0our Sun\u00a0in common, a\u00a0stellar member\u00a0of the\u00a0Milky Way\u00a0that ejects energetic particles which create\u00a0auroras. Far behind the middle of the\u00a0auroral arch\u00a0is the\u00a0Big Dipper. Even farther in the distance is the\u00a0Andromeda galaxy\u00a0-- the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The\u00a0featured image\u00a0was taken about a month ago from\u00a0Kval\u00f8ya\u00a0in northern\u00a0Norway.Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"milky way versus aurora\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Milky Way versus Aurora\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a  smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-17T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-16T14:03:43-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Milky Way versus Aurora\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 17<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg\" onMouseOver=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960_annotated.jpg';\"\nonMouseOut=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg';\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg\" name=imagename1 alt=\"A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a  smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Milky Way versus Aurora </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/guillaume_scheib/\">Guillaume Scheib</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way Galaxy</a>, an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/planet-earth-through-the-ages/\">our Earth</a>\u00a0and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2004-november-12-missouris-green-ribbon-sky/\">auroral ribbon</a>\u00a0that is only hours old, composed of energized\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/articles/zc86m39#zygjdp3\">atoms and molecules</a>, and is close by -- right in\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/properties-of-earths-upper-atmosphere/\">Earth's upper atmosphere</a>. These chance-superposed\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-30-the-surface-of-titan-from-huygens/\">titan</a>s have\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-june-15-prominences-and-filaments-on-the-active-sun/\">our Sun</a>\u00a0in common, a\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/media?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpreview.redd.it%2Fthis-is-the-most-cute-thing-i-ever-seen-v0-mpx697lakcue1.jpg%3Fwidth%3D1080%26crop%3Dsmart%26auto%3Dwebp%26s%3Da5d18b145135737912b5caa61fcc56766726f6cb\">stellar member</a>\u00a0of the\u00a0Milky Way\u00a0that ejects energetic particles which create\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-10-in-green-company-aurora-over-norway/\">aurora</a>s. Far behind the middle of the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-january-13-arches-across-an-arctic-sky/\">auroral arch</a>\u00a0is the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/\">Big Dipper</a>. Even farther in the distance is the\u00a0<a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/11675/\">Andromeda galaxy</a>\u00a0-- the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfAZEgmIDIq/\">featured image</a>\u00a0was taken about a month ago from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/4VlzJh3WBGU\">Kval\u00f8ya</a>\u00a0in northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway\">Norway</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-16-ngc-7129-star-formation-with-jets-from-webb/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261117/html"}